analysis:
The regular expression used to validate emails is vulnerable to Regular Expression Denial of Service (ReDoS) due to overlapping and nested quantifiers (`([-.\w]*[0-9a-zA-Z])*`). An attacker can send a specially crafted email string (e.g. `a` followed by 100 periods/hyphens, and then a non-matching character) which causes catastrophic backtracking in Python's `re` module, consuming excessive CPU resources and causing a Denial of Service (DoS) for the entire application.

**Conceptual Exploit Payload:**
PUT /users/v1/name1/email
{
  "email": "a...................................................................................!"
}

